--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -2,6 +2,8 @@
 
 ## Unreleased
 
+- C++ member calls resolve through the receiver's declared or inferred type: a local (`Engine e;`, `Engine e(1);`, `Engine e{};`), a parameter (`Pump* p`, `const Pump& p`), a global, a member of a typed struct (`car.engine.start()`, `cp->pump->start()`, a bare member name inside a method, `this->`), `auto` from `Engine()`, `new Engine`, `std::make_unique<Pump>()` or a cast, smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr<Pump>`), qualified and templated type names, `using` and `typedef` aliases, and base classes. The call reaches that class's method with certainty instead of a guess among every class with a `start`; a receiver whose type cannot be told stays a guess. A member declared in its class stands for its out-of-line definition, and `Type.name` no longer reports a tie between them. Schema 25; C and C++ files re-parse once.
+
 - `callers` of a C function-pointer member lists the lines storing a function in it (`.irq_mask = foo_mask,`) as their own group after the call sites, `stores a function in it (not calls): N`, no longer among the references that are not calls, and they no longer make the answer read as incomplete. `callers` of a function counts the documentation lines naming it after the verdict (`plus N in docs (not code, not listed)`) and never lists them.
 - The SubagentStart brief no longer carries the swarm board protocol line by default: in A/B runs it gave parallel subagents nothing and cost more. `[hooks] swarm_board = true` in `config.toml` brings it back. The parent packet and parent read lines stay.
 
