+**C++ member calls.** A call through a receiver (`e.start()`, `p->start()`) stores the receiver
+as a type path too (`ts_ref.rtype`, `C:Car|engine|start`); C++ type names are one namespace,
+`C:Engine`, whether a class, struct or typedef. The base is the declared type of a local,
+parameter (pointer, reference or value) or global, with `std::unique_ptr<T>`, `shared_ptr` and
+`optional` giving `T`; or the type an initializer says for `auto` (`Engine()`, `new Engine`,
+`Engine{}`, `std::make_unique<Pump>()`, the casts); `this`, or a name declared nowhere in a
+method, is a member of the method's class. Fields on the way give the next type from the class's
+own members (`meta.t`), through typedefs and `using` aliases. The call then reaches that
+class's member with certainty: its definition before its in-class declaration (a member
+declared in its class is a declaration, `meta.d`), the class's own before a base class's (base
+clauses are `impl` references). A receiver whose type cannot be told, or whose class and bases
+have no member of that name (a library type), is scored by name as before and stays a guess.
+Headers named `.h` are parsed as C. Schema 25 re-parses C and C++ files once.
+
